/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*----------------------------- File sort.dox ------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_sort */
/** \defgroup theory_sort Theory of sorting
 \ingroup area_sort

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/sort` realize the sorts of the book,
 i.e., the elementary ones of section 2.7, Quicksort of section 12.2,
 heapsort of section 10.1.4 and Shell Sort of section 15.1, and
 many beyond it, from the internal sorts of Knuth to the quicksorts, the
 merge sorts and the radix sorts engineered for the memory and for the vector
 registers of today, of which `sort()` and `stableSort()` of `Sort.h` choose
 one for each input. This page collects what the headers take for granted:
 the definitions and the notation shared by all of them (see
 \ref theory_sort_notation), the lower bound that the sorts by comparisons
 cannot beat (see \ref theory_sort_lower), and, for each family of
 algorithms, the property on which its correctness rests and the argument
 behind its bound (from \ref theory_sort_elementary to
 \ref theory_sort_other), with a table that compares them all (see
 \ref theory_sort_table). The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the
 page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the
 section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_sort_notation Definitions and notation

 A sort rearranges a vector \f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$ of items so that
 \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \le A[ i ] \f$ for each \f$ 0 < i < n \f$, where \f$ \le \f$ is
 a total preorder given by the comparator `less` of the call (the relation
 \f$ a < b \f$ being `less( a , b )`); two items are <em>equal</em> when
 neither is smaller than the other, which need not make them the same item. A
 sort is <em>stable</em> (section 12.5 of the book) if the equal items keep the
 order that they had, which matters when the items are records sorted by one of
 their fields, and it is <em>in place</em> if it uses \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ extra
 items, or \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ extra words for a stack. A sort <em>by
 comparisons</em> learns about the items only through `less`, and works
 therefore with any order; the sorts <em>by digits</em> (counting,
 pigeonhole, radix) read the keys as integers or strings instead, and are not
 bound by the lower bound of the sorts by comparisons (see \ref
 theory_sort_lower).

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$</td>
     <td>the number of items and the vector that holds them, with
         \f$ A[ \mathit{lo} .. \mathit{hi} - 1 ] \f$ a part of it</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ I( A ) \f$</td>
     <td>the inversions of \f$ A \f$, the pairs \f$ i < j \f$ with
         \f$ A[ j ] < A[ i ] \f$; \f$ 0 \le I( A ) \le n ( n - 1 ) / 2 \f$,
         and \f$ n ( n - 1 ) / 4 \f$ on average over the permutations
         (<tt>countInversions()</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ r \f$, \f$ \ell_1 , \ldots , \ell_r \f$</td>
     <td>the runs of \f$ A \f$, its maximal non-decreasing (or strictly
         decreasing) stretches, and their lengths</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ H \f$</td>
     <td>the entropy \f$ \sum_i ( \ell_i / n ) \log_2( n / \ell_i ) \f$ of
         the lengths of the runs, between 0 (one run) and
         \f$ \log_2 n \f$ (\f$ n \f$ runs of one item)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the number of distinct keys, or the range \f$ [ 0 , k ) \f$ of
         integer keys</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ w \f$</td>
     <td>the bytes (or bits) of a key read by a radix sort</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ D \f$</td>
     <td>the total length of the distinguishing prefixes of a set of
         strings, the shortest prefix of each string that tells it from all
         the others</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \sigma \f$</td>
     <td>the size of the alphabet of the strings, 256 for the bytes</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ W \f$</td>
     <td>the lanes of a vector register, 4 to 16 keys</td></tr>
 </table>

 Each sort ends, at the level of the audits [see `Contract.h`], with the
 check of `sortedPermutation()` of `Sorted.h`: the output is sorted and holds
 the items of the input, which a copy sorted by `std::sort` and compared
 item by item through the equivalence of `less` tells in
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$.

 \section theory_sort_lower The lower bound of the sorts by comparisons

 <em>Theorem 1 (the decision tree, section 2.7.4 of the book).</em> Any
 sort by comparisons makes, on some input of \f$ n \f$ distinct items, at
 least
 \f[
   \lceil \log_2 n! \rceil \ge n \log_2 n - n \log_2 e
   \approx n \log_2 n - 1.443 \, n \tag{1}
 \f]
 comparisons, and as many, but for a term \f$ O( n ) \f$, on average over the
 permutations of the input.

 Indeed, the comparisons that a sort makes form a binary tree, each node a
 comparison and each leaf an output; the \f$ n! \f$ orders of distinct items
 need \f$ n! \f$ different outputs, and a binary tree with \f$ n! \f$ leaves
 has height at least \f$ \log_2 n! \f$ and average depth of a leaf at least
 \f$ \log_2 n! \f$ too. The inequality of (1) follows from
 \f$ n! \ge ( n / e )^n \f$. Hence merge sort and heapsort, with
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ comparisons in the worst case, and Quicksort on
 average, are optimal up to a constant factor; the merge insertion of Ford
 and Johnson (`mergeInsertionSort()` of `KnuthSorts.h`) is within
 \f$ 0.03 \, n \f$ comparisons of (1), and for \f$ n \le 11 \f$ it meets it.

 The same argument gives finer bounds when the input is restricted. If the
 input is known to consist of \f$ r \f$ runs of lengths
 \f$ \ell_1 , \ldots , \ell_r \f$, the possible outputs are the ways of
 interleaving them, \f$ n! / ( \ell_1! \cdots \ell_r! ) \f$, and the bound
 becomes
 \f[
   \log_2 \frac{n!}{\ell_1! \cdots \ell_r!} \ge n H - O( n ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 which is 0 on a sorted input and (1) on \f$ n \f$ runs of one item; the
 merge sorts of `RunSorts.h` meet (2) up to \f$ O( n ) \f$ (see
 \ref theory_sort_merge). Likewise, with \f$ k \f$ distinct keys, each
 repeated \f$ n / k \f$ times, the bound is \f$ n \log_2 k - O( n ) \f$,
 which the partitions by three ways meet (see \ref theory_sort_quick). The
 sorts by digits escape (1), since a single step of theirs, the address
 \f$ B[ \mathit{key} ] \f$ of an array of counts, has \f$ k \f$ outcomes
 instead of 2; they are linear when the keys are short (see
 \ref theory_sort_digits).

 \section theory_sort_elementary The elementary sorts and the inversions

 The elementary sorts of section 2.7 keep a sorted prefix
 \f$ A[ 0 .. i - 1 ] \f$ and extend it by one item at each step. Selection
 Sort (`selectionSort()` of `SelectionSort.h`) appends the minimum of the
 rest, whatever the input, with \f$ n ( n - 1 ) / 2 \f$ comparisons and at
 most \f$ n - 1 \f$ swaps. Insertion Sort (`insertionSort()` of
 `InsertionSort.h`) inserts \f$ A[ i ] \f$ in the prefix, moving right the
 items larger than it, and its cost depends on the input as follows.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the inversions).</em> Insertion Sort makes exactly
 \f$ I( A ) \f$ moves and at most \f$ I( A ) + n - 1 \f$ comparisons; any
 sort that only swaps adjacent items makes at least \f$ I( A ) \f$ swaps, and
 hence
 \f[
   \frac{n ( n - 1 )}{4} \tag{3}
 \f]
 on average over the permutations.

 In fact, the item \f$ A[ i ] \f$ moves past exactly the items of the prefix
 larger than it, i.e., the inversions \f$ ( j , i ) \f$ with \f$ j < i \f$,
 and each test of the inner loop but the last one is followed by a move. A
 swap of two adjacent items changes \f$ I( A ) \f$ by exactly one, and a
 permutation and its reverse together have \f$ n ( n - 1 ) / 2 \f$
 inversions, which gives (3). Insertion Sort is thus \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ on
 an input with \f$ O( n ) \f$ inversions, and with its simple loop it is the
 fastest on a few items, which makes it the base case of all the hybrid
 sorts of the area. The sorts by adjacent exchanges of `ExchangeSorts.h`
 (bubble, cocktail shaker, odd-even transposition, gnome) and the binary and
 list insertion sorts of `KnuthSorts.h` are all bound by (3) in their moves
 or swaps.

 Shell Sort (`shellSort()` of `ShellSort.h`, section 15.1) escapes (3) by
 exchanging far items first: a pass with gap \f$ h \f$ is an insertion sort
 of the \f$ h \f$ interleaved subsequences, and a swap at distance \f$ h \f$
 may remove up to \f$ 2 h - 1 \f$ inversions. A vector that is
 \f$ h \f$-sorted stays \f$ h \f$-sorted after a pass with a smaller gap,
 which is why the last pass, with gap 1, finds few inversions. The time
 depends on the gaps, which the header compares: \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ in the
 worst case with the gaps \f$ n / 2^j \f$ of Shell, whose common factors
 leave the odd and the even places apart until the last pass;
 \f$ O( n^{3/2} ) \f$ with \f$ 2^j - 1 \f$ (Hibbard) and with
 \f$ ( 3^j - 1 ) / 2 \f$ (the book); \f$ O( n \log^2 n ) \f$ with all the
 \f$ 2^i 3^j \f$ (Pratt), since a vector that is 2-sorted and 3-sorted needs
 a single pass of adjacent swaps to be 1-sorted.

 \section theory_sort_merge Merge sorts and runs

 Merge Sort (`mergeSort()` of `MergeSort.h`, section 2.7.3) splits the vector
 into two halves, sorts them recursively and merges them; the merge of two
 sorted vectors of \f$ n_1 \f$ and \f$ n_2 \f$ items takes at most
 \f$ n_1 + n_2 - 1 \f$ comparisons, since each comparison outputs an item,
 and it is stable if the left item goes first on a tie. Hence, in
 the worst case,
 \f[
   T( n ) = T( \lceil n / 2 \rceil ) + T( \lfloor n / 2 \rfloor ) + n - 1
   = n \lceil \log_2 n \rceil - 2^{\lceil \log_2 n \rceil} + 1 , \tag{4}
 \f]
 within \f$ n \f$ comparisons of (1), at the price of a buffer of
 \f$ n \f$ items. The bottom-up version (`mergeSortBottomUp()`) merges the
 runs of lengths \f$ 1 , 2 , 4 , \ldots \f$ with no recursion, and a merge
 also counts the inversions, since an item taken from the right half
 overtakes all the items left in the left one (`countInversions()`).

 The natural merge sorts merge the runs that the input already has instead
 of the halves, and the order of the merges decides the cost: merging two
 runs costs their total length, so that a sequence of merges is a binary
 tree over the runs whose cost is the sum of
 \f$ \ell_i \cdot \mathrm{depth}( i ) \f$, as in the codes of Huffman, and
 the best tree costs between \f$ n H \f$ and \f$ n H + n \f$. Timsort keeps
 the runs on a stack whose lengths grow from the top at least as the
 Fibonacci numbers, merging when the rule fails; the stack has thus
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ runs, and its merges cost \f$ O( n + n H ) \f$
 comparisons (Auger, Jugé, Nicaud and Pivoteau). Powersort (`powerSort()`
 of `RunSorts.h`) merges along a tree that is nearly the one of the
 midpoints: the boundary between two adjacent runs has the power of the
 first bit where their midpoints, as fractions of \f$ n \f$, differ, and a
 run on the stack is merged when its boundary has a larger power than the
 new one; this tree costs at most \f$ n H + 2 n \f$ (Munro and Wild), within
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ of (2). It extends the runs shorter than
 \f$ \mathit{minrun} \f$ (24 items) by binary insertion, and gallops
 (an exponential search, then a binary one) when one run keeps winning, so
 that two runs one after the other merge in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$
 comparisons. The strand sort of `StrandSort.h` builds runs of its own,
 the strands being as many as the items of a longest strictly decreasing
 subsequence.

 \section theory_sort_quick Quicksort and its variants

 Quicksort (`quickSort()` of `QuickSort.h`, section 12.2) partitions the part
 around a pivot \f$ x \f$, the smaller items before it and the others after
 it, and sorts the two sides recursively; the partition costs \f$ n - 1 \f$
 comparisons, and the sides may be anything from 0 to \f$ n - 1 \f$ items.

 <em>Theorem 3 (Quicksort on average).</em> With the pivot of a part equally
 likely to be any of its items, e.g., on a random permutation or with a
 pivot chosen at random, the expected number of comparisons \f$ C( n ) \f$
 satisfies
 \f[
   C( n ) = n - 1 + \frac{2}{n} \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} C( j )
   = 2 ( n + 1 ) H_n - 4 n \approx 1.386 \, n \log_2 n , \tag{5}
 \f]
 \f$ H_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} 1 / i \f$ the harmonic numbers.

 Indeed, the pivot is the \f$ j + 1 \f$-th smallest with probability
 \f$ 1 / n \f$, and leaves sides of \f$ j \f$ and \f$ n - 1 - j \f$ items,
 which are again random; this gives the recurrence. Subtracting the one of
 \f$ n - 1 \f$ multiplied by \f$ n - 1 \f$ from the one of \f$ n \f$ multiplied
 by \f$ n \f$ gives \f$ n C( n ) = ( n + 1 ) C( n - 1 ) + 2 ( n - 1 ) \f$, and
 dividing by \f$ n ( n + 1 ) \f$ telescopes to (5). Equivalently, the items of
 ranks \f$ i < j \f$ are compared if and only if one of them is the first pivot
 among the ranks \f$ i , \ldots , j \f$, which happens with probability
 \f$ 2 / ( j - i + 1 ) \f$, and the sum of these probabilities is (5). In the
 worst case, a pivot that is always the smallest item (the first item of a
 sorted vector) gives \f$ n ( n - 1 ) / 2 \f$ comparisons and \f$ n - 1 \f$
 levels of recursion.

 The variants of the area act on the three weak points of (5): the constant,
 the worst case and the equal keys. The pivot as the median of three, or of
 three medians of three (the ninther of Tukey), lowers the constant of (5) to
 about \f$ 1.188 \f$ and makes the sorted inputs a good case
 (`quickSortTuned()`, `pdqSort()`). The introsort of Musser (inside
 `pdqSort()`) bounds the depth of the recursion by
 \f$ 2 \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor \f$ and sorts a part that goes deeper by
 heapsort, so that the worst case is \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$;
 recurring on the smaller side and looping on the larger keeps the stack within
 \f$ \log_2 n \f$ parts, since each recursive call at least halves the part. A
 partition by three ways keeps the items equal to the pivot apart
 (`threeWayQuickSort()`, Algorithm 5.4 of chapter 5, Sorting Atomic Items,
 of Ferragina's book, with a random pivot), or skips a run of them when
 the pivot equals the item before the part (`pdqSort()` of Peters), so that the
 keys equal to a pivot are never examined again, and \f$ k \f$ distinct keys
 cost \f$ O( n \log k ) \f$, the lower bound for them (see \ref
 theory_sort_lower). Two pivots split the part into three in one pass
 (`dualPivotQuickSort()` of Yaroslavskiy, section 5.3.5 of the same
 chapter), with about \f$ 1.9 \, n \ln n \f$
 comparisons against the \f$ 2 \, n \ln n \f$ of (5) but fewer scans of the
 memory (Wild and Nebel). Finally, the speed of a partition on a modern
 processor depends less on its comparisons than on the branches that it
 mispredicts: the BlockQuicksort of Edelkamp and Weiss (inside `pdqSort()`) and
 `vectorQuickSort()` of `VectorQuickSort.h`, which compares \f$ W \f$ keys with
 the pivot in one instruction and writes them with a compressed store, make the
 partition free of branches on the keys.

 Samplesort (`sampleSort()` of `SampleSort.h`) generalizes the pivot to
 \f$ k - 1 \f$ splitters drawn from a sample of \f$ \alpha k - 1 \f$ items,
 and distributes the items into \f$ k \f$ buckets by a search in a tree of
 the splitters with no branch (Sanders and Winkel), in place by blocks
 (Axtmann, Witt, Ferizovic and Sanders). With \f$ k = 256 \f$ there are
 about \f$ \log_{256}( n / 16 ) \f$ levels of distribution, each reading and
 writing every item twice; the expected comparisons are
 \f$ n \log_2 n + O( n ) \f$, since each level compares an item with
 \f$ \log_2 k \f$ splitters, and the sample makes a bucket much larger than
 \f$ n / k \f$ unlikely.

 The learned sort (`learnedSort()` of `LearnedSort.h`, after Kristo et al.
 and Ferragina and Odorisio) keeps the splitters of samplesort but replaces
 the search among them by a model of the cumulative distribution function
 \f$ F \f$ of the keys, \f$ F( x ) \f$ the fraction of the keys not larger
 than \f$ x \f$. A distribution into \f$ k \f$ buckets is balanced when the
 bucket of \f$ x \f$ is \f$ \lfloor k F( x ) \rfloor \f$, and the splitters
 at the quantiles \f$ i / k \f$ of a sample approximate it within the error
 of the empirical CDF \f$ F_s \f$ of the sample, which is bounded whatever
 \f$ F \f$ is.

 <em>Lemma 4 (Dvoretzky, Kiefer and Wolfowitz, with the constant of
 Massart).</em> For a sample of \f$ s \f$ keys drawn independently from
 \f$ F \f$, and every \f$ \varepsilon > 0 \f$,
 \f[
   \Pr\Bigl[ \sup_x | F_s( x ) - F( x ) | > \varepsilon \Bigr]
   \le 2 e^{-2 s \varepsilon^2} . \tag{6}
 \f]

 The proof is in the paper of Massart. With \f$ s = 16 k \f$ and
 \f$ \varepsilon \f$ a small multiple of \f$ 1 / \sqrt{s} \f$, (6) gives every
 bucket \f$ n / k \pm 2 \varepsilon n \f$ keys with high probability, as the
 sample of samplesort does. The model evaluates the number of splitters less
 than \f$ x \f$ by a grid of cells of equal width \f$ w \f$, a power of 2, on
 the range of the sample: the cell of \f$ x \f$ is a shift of
 \f$ x - S_{\min} \f$, each cell keeps the number of splitters before it (a
 histogram of the sample and its prefix sums), and if no cell holds more than
 \f$ c_{\max} \f$ splitters the bucket is that count plus \f$ c_{\max} \f$
 comparisons with no branch, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time instead of the
 \f$ \log_2 k \f$ dependent loads of a search. The grid fails exactly where the
 keys are dense with respect to their mean on the range (the laws with a heavy
 tail, the clusters far apart) or the sample repeats its quantiles (few
 distinct keys); both are seen on the sample before any key moves, and the sort
 then falls back to samplesort. When the model fits, the classification and the
 moves cost \f$ O( n ) \f$ and the sorts of the buckets
 \f$ O( n \log( n / k ) ) \f$ in expectation.

 \section theory_sort_heap Heaps and selection by trees

 A max-heap on a vector has
 \f$ A[ \lfloor ( i - 1 ) / 2 \rfloor ] \ge A[ i ] \f$ for each \f$ i > 0 \f$,
 and its maximum in \f$ A[ 0 ] \f$ (section 10.1.4). `maxHeapRestore()` of
 `Heapsort.h` moves an item down from a node of height \f$ m \f$ in
 \f$ O( m ) \f$, and `heapBuild()` calls it on the internal nodes from the last
 one up; since at most \f$ \lceil n / 2^{m+1} \rceil \f$ nodes have height
 \f$ m \f$, the build costs
 \f[
   \sum_{m \ge 0} \Bigl\lceil \frac{n}{2^{m+1}} \Bigr\rceil O( m )
   = O\Bigl( n \sum_{m \ge 0} \frac{m}{2^m} \Bigr) = O( n ) , \tag{7}
 \f]
 the series being 2. Heapsort then swaps the maximum with the last place of
 the heap and restores it \f$ n - 1 \f$ times, \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ in all
 and in place, with about \f$ 2 n \log_2 n \f$ comparisons; it is the
 fallback of the introsorts because its bound holds on every input. The
 tournament sort of `KnuthSorts.h` plays a knockout on a complete tree of
 the items and replays only the path of the winner,
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$ comparisons for each item, and the tree
 sort of `TreeSort.h` inserts the items in a search tree and reads them
 back in symmetric order, \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ on a balanced tree and
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ on a sorted input with no balance.

 \section theory_sort_digits Sorts by digits

 Counting Sort (`countingSort()` of `CountingSort.h`, section 2.7.5) counts
 the occurrences of each integer key in \f$ [ 0 , k ) \f$ and writes them
 back in order, in \f$ \Theta( n + k ) \f$ time; Pigeonhole Sort
 (`pigeonholeSort()` of `PigeonholeSort.h`, exercise 4.11) appends each item
 to the list of its key and concatenates the lists, which carries the
 values along and is stable. A radix sort applies a stable distribution to
 each digit of the keys, and its correctness rests on the following.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the least significant digit first).</em> If the keys have
 \f$ w \f$ digits, and each pass distributes the items stably by one digit,
 from the least significant to the most, then after the pass on the digit
 \f$ j \f$ the items are sorted by the number formed by their digits
 \f$ 0 , \ldots , j \f$.

 Indeed, after the pass on \f$ j \f$ two items with different digits
 \f$ j \f$ are in the order of those digits, and two with the same digit
 \f$ j \f$ keep the order that they had, i.e., by induction, the order of
 their digits \f$ 0 , \ldots , j - 1 \f$. Hence `radixSort()` of
 `RadixSort.h`, with bytes as digits, takes \f$ O( w ( n + 256 ) ) \f$,
 linear for keys of a fixed width, and skips the bytes in which no two keys
 differ; its keys make the order of the numbers that of unsigned integers
 (the sign bit flipped, and all the bits of a negative floating point
 number). The sorts from the most significant digit (`americanFlagSort()`,
 `msdRadixSort()`) distribute by the first digit and recur on each bucket,
 reading only the digits that tell the keys apart; on strings they read
 \f$ D \f$ characters, which any sort of strings must read, and spend
 \f$ O( n \log \sigma ) \f$ beyond them on an alphabet of \f$ \sigma \f$
 characters. `americanFlagSort()` permutes the items in place, not along the
 cycles of the permutation, whose every step waits for the load of the one
 before, but by rounds in which each item of a bucket is swapped to the next
 free place of its own, so that the loads of a round are independent, and
 leaves the buckets of fewer than `kFlagSmall` items to a quicksort, whose
 partitions in the vector registers cost less than the passes by a byte that
 they replace. The multikey quicksort of Bentley and Sedgewick
 (`multikeyQuickSort()`, of chapter 7, Sorting Strings, of Ferragina's book)
 partitions by three ways on one character at a time,
 \f$ O( D + n \log n ) \f$ comparisons of characters, and `prefixKeySort()`
 of `StringSort.h` turns 8 characters at a time into integer keys sorted in
 the vector registers.

 \section theory_sort_other Networks, other measures and external memory

 A sorting network (`SortingNetworks.h`) is a fixed sequence of comparators
 \f$ ( i , j ) \f$, each putting the smaller of \f$ A[ i ] \f$ and
 \f$ A[ j ] \f$ in \f$ A[ i ] \f$, whatever the input; the comparators of a
 round touch disjoint places and run in parallel. Its correctness rests on
 the 0-1 principle of Knuth: a network sorts every input if and only if it
 sorts the \f$ 2^n \f$ inputs of zeros and ones, since a comparator commutes
 with every non-decreasing function of the items, and an input out of order
 at the end gives, through a threshold, an input of zeros and ones out of
 order. The bitonic sorter and the merge exchange of Batcher take
 \f$ O( \log^2 n ) \f$ rounds and \f$ O( n \log^2 n ) \f$ comparators, more
 than (1), which they pay for with their parallelism; the network of
 `vectorQuickSort()` sorts \f$ 16 W \f$ keys in the registers in this way.

 A file larger than the memory is sorted in two phases (`ExternalSort.h`,
 sections 5.4.1 and 5.4.2 of Knuth). The replacement selection keeps \f$ M \f$
 items in a heap and outputs the least that can extend the current run; on
 random input the runs are about \f$ 2 M \f$ long, twice the \f$ M \f$ of
 sorting one memory load at a time (the snowplow argument of Knuth). The
 polyphase merge deals the runs on \f$ T - 1 \f$ tapes in a generalized
 Fibonacci distribution, padded with empty runs, so that each phase merges
 until one tape is empty and that tape becomes the next output; with three
 tapes each item takes part in about \f$ \log_\phi r \f$ merges, \f$ \phi \f$
 the golden ratio, and no tape is ever only copied.

